# Introduction

## Purpose of the Assignment

Modern marketing research depends on **latent constructs**: brand attitude, customer satisfaction, purchase intent, perceived value, and technology anxiety.
None of these can be observed directly.
Marketers capture them through survey items, and then must demonstrate that those items actually measure what the research team claims they measure.
Confirmatory factor analysis (CFA) is the statistical framework for making that case.

This study note documents my completion of the UCLA seminar *Confirmatory Factor Analysis in R with lavaan*.
The seminar uses the Statistics Anxiety Questionnaire (SAQ-8), a set of eight survey items.
I treat the SAQ-8 as a stand-in for any consumer or stakeholder scale a digital marketing team might deploy, such as a customer-experience battery or an adoption-barrier index.
The goal is a reusable reference: the code, the logic, and the interpretation rules I will need when I build or audit a measurement model in future research, including my culminating project.

All code in this note is written step by step, with each operation shown on its own line, so that every stage of the workflow is visible and reproducible.

## Confirmatory Factor Analysis and Exploratory Factor Analysis

Exploratory factor analysis (EFA) lets the data suggest how many factors exist and which items load on which factor.
It is a discovery tool, appropriate when theory is thin or a scale is new.
Confirmatory factor analysis (CFA) begins with a **theory-driven measurement model**, in which the researcher specifies in advance the number of factors, which items load on which factors, and which parameters are fixed or free.
The data are then used to test whether that specification reproduces the observed covariances adequately.

| Feature | EFA | CFA |
|------------------------|------------------------|------------------------|
| Purpose | Discover structure | Test a hypothesized structure |
| Item-factor assignment | Estimated for every item on every factor | Specified by the researcher |
| Cross-loadings | Allowed by default | Fixed to zero unless specified |
| Model-fit testing | Limited | Central (chi-square, CFI, TLI, RMSEA) |
| Typical marketing use | Early scale development | Validating a scale before using it in segmentation, brand tracking, or regression |

